  "# A test that ends the JVM is not a failing test

## The finding

`kotoba-lang/amu` runs its suite through one `run-tests` call over 128
namespaces. `kotoba.compiler.cli` ends an error path by calling `*exit*`, which
is `System/exit` unless something rebinds it. A test that reached that path
without binding it did not fail — it ended the process, so `run-tests` never
returned, no totals were printed, and every namespace after it was simply never
asked.

**Measured 2026-08-17: 11 of 128 namespaces ran.** The output contained no
failures, because nothing was left alive to report one.

This is the failure shape ADR-2608136000 names — a check that could not run
returning what a check that ran and passed returns — with the additional
property that it takes the rest of the suite with it. Three pin-advance commits
in that repository cite the run as \"0 failures and 0 errors\". The number was
true. It meant that 117 namespaces had not been asked, and the reader who wrote
it did so twice before checking.

## What the darkness was hiding

With the runner able to finish, the same host reported 1,027 tests, 7,083
assertions, 10 failures and 79 errors. Both groups had a single cause:

- **79 errors: one host-configuration mismatch.** `kotoba.wasm.tools` pins
  wasm-tools 1.243.0; the machine had 1.256.0 first on `PATH`. With the pinned
  version resolving, 79 became 0 and the assertion count rose to 7,915 — those
  tests had been erroring before they could assert.
- **10 failures: real drift.** Six were the same three counts copied into five
  assertions across two namespaces, stale since a conformance case was added.
  Four were `thrown-with-msg?` matchers against prose that had moved into a
  structured `:code`, reporting working fail-closed paths as broken.

None were new. All had been failing for as long as nothing could reach them.

## Decision

**A test runner must be able to say how much of itself ran.** Three parts, all
landed in amu:

1. **Make the exit rebindable and bind it.** `cli_test` binds `*exit*` for
   every test in the namespace to throw. A test that means to observe an exit
   status rebinds it itself and shadows the fixture; for any other test an exit
   is a defect and now surfaces as one.

2. **Hold the suite as data and report coverage.** The runner prints how many
   namespaces it will run, counts how many it finished, and prints
   `COMPLETE -- N of M`. Absence of failures is not evidence of success unless
   something states how far the run got.

3. **Say so when the process leaves early.** A shutdown hook prints
   `INCOMPLETE -- finished N of M` if the JVM exits mid-run. `System/exit`
   cannot be intercepted, but it can be made loud.

**The cheap dynamic fingerprint is an exit code that is neither 0 nor 1.** A
runner exits 0 or 1; amu's suite exited 70.

## Exposure

`scripts/exit-exposure.cljs` reports where a test requires a namespace whose
source can call `System/exit`, and whether that exit goes through a rebindable
dynamic var or is raw.

```sh
nbb scripts/exit-exposure.cljs orgs/kotoba-lang
```

Measured 2026-08-17 over the checked-out tree:

| org | repositories with src/ and test/ | exposed |
|---|---:|---:|
| kotoba-lang | 1,910 | 19 |
| cloud-itonami | 1,546 | 15 |
| etzhayyim | 117 | 5 |
| network-awai | 26 | 7 |
| gftdcojp | 18 | 1 |
| **total** | **3,617** | **47** |

**Exposure is not vulnerability.** The report states a precondition: a test can
reach a namespace that can exit. Whether any test does is what running the
suite answers. It is offered as the list worth checking, not as a defect count,
and a repository that is not checked out does not appear at all.

amu itself is still listed, and correctly: `kotoba.compiler.cli` is guarded
now, but `fuel-estimate`, `lang-conformance` and `lang-native-conformance`
still exit raw.

## Consequences

- The first drift the finished suite caught on main arrived within hours:
  `deps.edn` had advanced three native-boundary pins without the guard that
  exists to make that advance deliberate. The ratchet had been bypassable
  without anyone choosing to bypass it.
- A host that carries an unpinned tool version turns into 79 errors rather than
  a silent stop. That is louder and better, and it means CI images without
  wasm-tools will now say so.
- Prefer a structured `:code` over prose in `thrown-with-msg?`. The message is
  something a provider may reword; the code is the contract.
- Prefer deriving a count from the thing it describes over copying a literal.
  The same three numbers appeared in five places here and rotted unevenly."}]
